(Description)
- Given the head of a linked list, reverse the nodes of the list k at a time, and return the modified list.
- k is a positive integer and is less than or equal to the length of the linked list. 
  If the number of nodes is not a multiple of k then left-out nodes, in the end, should remain as it is.

(You may not alter the values in the list's nodes, only nodes themselves may be changed.)

--- Solution ---
class Solution {
    public ListNode reverseKGroup(ListNode head, int k) {
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        dummy.next = head;

        ListNode groupPrev = dummy;

        while (true) {
            // Find the kth node of the current group
            ListNode kth = groupPrev;

            for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
                kth = kth.next;

                // Not enough nodes left
                if (kth == null) {
                    return dummy.next;
                }
            }

            // First node after the current group
            ListNode groupNext = kth.next;

            // Reverse the current group
            ListNode prev = groupNext;
            ListNode curr = groupPrev.next;

            while (curr != groupNext) {
                ListNode temp = curr.next;
                curr.next = prev;
                prev = curr;
                curr = temp;
            }

            // Reconnect the reversed group
            ListNode temp = groupPrev.next;
            groupPrev.next = kth;
            groupPrev = temp;
        }
    }
}

--- Approach ---
The idea of dummy node could make this problem to solve easily.
Because the dummy node keeps the position before the first node, when the number of nodes are not enough to 
multiple of the given number k, it could easily stops the loop, returning the next node of dummy.

(Reversing Process)
In the K-Group nodes, if the first node's next was set to the next group's node, moving the pointers
1. the node should become the next to reverse is what was last linked one.
2. the node should be set its next is right next node from current node.

If this process repeats until that two pointers become same node, the nodes in the K-Group reversed.

Then, it sets groupPrev node points to the previous node of next K-Group nodes, which is the last node of the current K-Group to repeat this process in the next group.
